/*Q2. Write a Java program to implement a simple Bank Account Management System using a class, constructor, instance methods, and the
this keyword.
Complete the provided BankAccount class with the following instance variables:
• name (String): stores the account holder's name.
• balance (double): stores the current account balance.
Constructor
Implement a parameterized constructor BankAccount(String name, double balance) that initializes both instance variables using the
this keyword.
Methods
Implement the following methods in the BankAccount class:
• deposit(double amount): Adds the specified amount to the current balance and displays the updated balance.
• withdraw(double amount): Deducts the specified amount if sufficient balance is available. Otherwise, displays Insufficient balance
without changing the balance.
• displayBalance(): Displays the current account balance.
The BankAccount class must also implement main() to read the account details, create a BankAccount object, and process the specified
sequence of operations.
Input Format:
The first line contains a string representing the account holder's name.
• The second line contains a floating-point number representing the initial balance.
• The third line contains an integer g, representing the number of operations.
• Each of the next q lines contains one of the following operations:
L deposit <amount> — where <amount> is a floating-point number representing the amount to deposit.
2. withdraw <amount> — where <amount> is a floating-point number representing the amount to withdraw.
3. display — displays the current account balance.
Output Format:
For each operation, print the corresponding output immediately after it is processed:
• For a deposit operation:
Balance after deposit: <balance>
• For a withdraw operation:
Balance after withdrawal: <balance>
or
Insufficient balance
• For a display operation:
Current balance: <balance>
Note: Print all balance values (after deposit, after withdrawal, and current balance) rounded to two decimal places.*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
public class BankAccount {
    String name;
    double balance;
 
    BankAccount(String name, double balance) {
        this.name = name;
        this.balance = balance;
    }
 
    void deposit(double amount) {
        this.balance += amount;
        System.out.printf("Balance after deposit: %.2f%n", this.balance);
    }
 
    void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount <= this.balance) {
            this.balance -= amount;
            System.out.printf("Balance after withdrawal: %.2f%n", this.balance);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Insufficient balance");
        }
    }
 
    void displayBalance() {
        System.out.printf("Current balance: %.2f%n", this.balance);
    }
 
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        String name = sc.nextLine();
        double balance = Double.parseDouble(sc.nextLine().trim());
        int q = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
        BankAccount acc = new BankAccount(name, balance);
        for (int i = 0; i < q; i++) {
            String[] op = sc.nextLine().trim().split("\\s+");
            switch (op[0].toLowerCase()) {
                case "deposit":  acc.deposit(Double.parseDouble(op[1])); break;
                case "withdraw": acc.withdraw(Double.parseDouble(op[1])); break;
                case "display":  acc.displayBalance(); break;
            }
        }
        sc.close();
    }
}
